4. Pipe-write durations and scheduler evidence — writes-only capture record created 2026-11-15 09:48:31.585845 UTC; per-thread schedstat probe recorded at 10:20:43 UTC. Across 25,800 FFmpeg output writes, duration was median 0.058 ms, p95 0.153 ms, p99 0.256 ms, maximum 10.554 ms, none >50 ms; sustained pipe backpressure is unsupported. In the untraced 600-frame schedstat probe, 77/599 full-read intervals exceeded 60 ms (maximum 80.383947 ms). Summed runqueue delay averaged 1.469 ms on long gaps versus 1.273 ms otherwise; only 3/77 long gaps had any thread at >=5 ms. This does not support primary CPU runqueue delay; post-read `/proc` snapshots add observer cost to the next interval. Sources: `../build/reports/report_ffmpeg-stdout-write-trace-r2.3-r1.md` SHA-256 c0b6822692ce075774854ddcba5996d32e633382659e5767e00efe25fe6a1268, artifact `../build/artifacts/ffmpeg-stdout-write-trace-r2.3-r1.json` SHA-256 5dede62fa4529206a576e6241723f9c4c6b5aa4cd55eebbe45d23da3833fe49f; and `../build/reports/report_ffmpeg-thread-schedstat-r4.md` SHA-256 d7ac782c3f6bc118d8db096f18da8d326cba30b862ef732bb2f8ef9608632faa, correlation JSON `../build/artifacts/ffmpeg-thread-schedstat-correlation-r4.json` SHA-256 48d0a6517c05a9b40a377de87a358ee93ac12b70a44a47d04bfdafc44bedfb5a, capture JSON `../build/artifacts/ffmpeg-thread-syscall-schedstat-r4-final2.json` SHA-256 b64db8453b48b731dafbec8b33db5d40a0c577ab698ff29d204c2127e176cba8, and raw trace `../build/evidence/ffmpeg-thread-syscall-schedstat-r4-final2.strace` SHA-256 0de7b3e50ff49d676085ff34e36e6fad8b9fb36bcbd7200d9df251cb0b9e7037. The raw syscall trace timestamps span 2026-11-15 11:50:34.277292–11:51:05.307426 UTC. In that instrumented capture, 133/599 long gaps overlapped futex waits; syscall overlap is correlational and strace/schedstat observer overhead was not independently calibrated.

5. Futex pairing and flush ABBA — offline pairing analysis used the syscall trace above; it was not a new capture. The raw-pipe writer TID 863811 had 598 successful FUTEX_WAIT calls >=1 ms (592 >=10 ms), each with a positive same-address FUTEX_WAKE from leader TID 863769 temporally inside the wait. `FUTEX_WAKE` does not identify which waiter woke; the output writer may be idle awaiting upstream work. All 133 >60 ms gaps also overlap >=1 ms futex waits by each of TIDs 863769, 863808, and 863811. This is synchronization correlation, not causality. Offline sources: `evidence/ffmpeg-futex-wake-pairing-r5.log` SHA-256 b4da13d544b42b0acb8a1b79c2668ad1fa51af68f6bce1460ccb8f015562ceca and `artifacts/ffmpeg-futex-wake-pairing-r5.json` SHA-256 5c69e8b7f52201fef1ec9a4958bbd3e0b485b1a3f9ec58882847e0e1fdfc07f3. The sequential traced flush ABBA arms ran at 11:23:18, 11:23:49, 11:24:20, and 11:24:51 UTC; pooled >60 ms read gaps were 228/1,198 for default versus 227/1,198 with `-flush_packets 1`, while the flush maxima were worse (96.677 ms vs 90.374 ms). This does not show a repeatable flush effect. Sources: `../build/reports/report_ffmpeg-flush-abba-r3.md` SHA-256 e904cc3db831e98eb200e7bdd988b333082ccacfa4df4a85daa2410d94259ca2, `../build/artifacts/ffmpeg-flush-abba-r3.json` SHA-256 db777e8aa2d4b63155e5595cda47cfa863367805885c270f518df3dde7389671, and summary log `../build/evidence/ffmpeg-flush-abba-r3.log` SHA-256 7816977983473254c5fc01747f84a1d33d07b35869b3021a425128363ace0064.

6. Bounded r7 GDB sampling — monotonic capture span 23,296,446,952,165–23,328,487,932,400 ns; three successful snapshots, 63 watchdog triggers and 60 budget-suppressed triggers. The report says the rawvideo mux thread was in `write()` in all three stacks; the main thread was in condition waits in two and `sws_scale_frame()` in one. These are callsites only. GDB pauses perturbed read timing (65/599 >60 ms, max 1,098.139 ms); the 600 frames were read, then FFmpeg cleanup ended by SIGKILL and the child was reaped. No frame payload was retained. The snapshots do not prove the wait or scale call caused any AC-4 gap. Sources: `../build/reports/report_gdb-sampling-r7.md` SHA-256 d7412919734b279918b23d0ef265f44a1117a2cbabe1904ace84c834565d0fb8, `../build/evidence/gdb-sampling-r7.log` SHA-256 adb417c4888e65b16e13cb100370a4cb961307469bee7599696580bdf2ce24a9, and `../build/artifacts/ffmpeg-gdb-sampling-r7.json` SHA-256 c059e3000c236a6f05cb336fe9afbdecdadc393228f4b77c7f05ec5e029e4782.

7. Prior investigation lineage r1–r7 — prior packages and evidence chains are preserved in the run tree; the approved former r7 package, manifest, and cross-stage verdict were copied byte-for-byte before the canonical report/manifest are updated. The r1 hypothesis that source PTS could replace EOF was superseded when PTS semantics were established as SOE. Revisions r2–r4 bounded the divergence to the FFmpeg processing/output path as a suspect, rejected subscriber backlog and primary runqueue delay, and found the one-arm flush effect non-repeatable. Revision r5 recorded temporal futex correlation without causal identity; r6/r7 retained that uncertainty and bounded GDB use to callsite localization. This history is summarized cumulatively in `evidence/evidence-chain-r7.log` SHA-256 332fc786c22f458166b14dccbc4a338b2f9aedf761fbe6a9efa5c7602c700cdc and the archived package `reports/investigation-package-r7.md` SHA-256 35210e5f097b0ed2dc2b1607bdf9d97ea4b61b53eb5377526a8a0fe9f9181eb4. Its archived manifest is `artifacts/manifest-r7.json` SHA-256 ea45c0984b53ecd9e2fef5fb280c56fafd8cb369fb63f44f5e31a4cfdfbaf540; the approved immutable archived r7 verdict is `artifacts/investigation-review-r7-cross-stage.archive.json` SHA-256 a9dc61fa45dbb540e5adb2d3e2692f64c1a9f9091b3f952c85b3961728a4dd6c. The canonical verdict path is deliberately not used as r8 lineage input.

Inference (not an observed link): existing evidence is consistent with timing variation inside FFmpeg decode/filter/output pacing or synchronization after source-side timing, with host scheduling still possible. The exact FFmpeg submechanism is unknown. Separate captures and missing DQBUF sequence identity prevent same-frame source-to-output attribution; futex association and GDB callsites do not establish causation. Sustained pipe backpressure, subscriber backlog, a primary runqueue delay, source PTS as EOF, and a repeatable flush-option effect are unsupported by the available evidence. The required resolving observation is one same-frame timeline through V4L2 dequeue sequence/time, FFmpeg decode and filter completion, raw-pipe write boundaries/full-read completion, and per-thread scheduling for a >60 ms gap. No such observation is present in this handoff.
